# Compatibility

<p class="lede">
ppd {meta.version} is verified on macOS with Paseo {meta.verifiedPaseo}, desktop app and CLI. It relies on parts of
Paseo that are not a public API, and says so when one of them moves instead of blaming your plugin.
</p>

<h2 id="needs">What it needs</h2>

| Tool | For | Without it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Node {meta.node} | everything | |
| Paseo desktop app | the real compiler, evaluator and Lucide list in `ppd check`, the web UI, `agentTimeline` | `ppd check` runs as a labeled approximation; `ppd web` needs a web UI build (`--web-dist`) |
| Paseo CLI | `ppd daemon`, `install`, `dev`, `paseo`, the fake agent | no dev daemon |
| Google Chrome, or Playwright's Chromium | `ppd web`, `PaseoWeb` | no screenshots |
| `react-native` devDependency, or `HERMESC` and `HERMES` | the Hermes compile and VM checks | those two checks skip |
| adb | `ppd android` | no phone |

macOS and Linux. On Linux (CI), there is no Paseo app: daemon tests skip and `ppd check` is an approximation.
Windows is not supported.

<h2 id="host-dependencies">React Native, React and dependency updates</h2>

Paseo supplies React Native, React, the plugin SDK, TanStack Query and Zod at runtime. Their development
dependencies give your editor and TypeScript a local API surface. Updating them does not replace the app's
runtime or renderer. Match the oldest Paseo app you support, rather than selecting the newest React Native
release independently. The manifest's `requirements.paseo` covers both the daemon and each connected app;
it has no separate React Native key. SDK peer ranges constrain package installation, not verified host/API
compatibility.

React Native's [versioning policy](https://reactnative.dev/releases/versioning-policy) permits breaking changes
in `0.x` minor releases. Treat a new minor series as a migration. Review React, React Native and SDK versions
together; do not bypass conflicting peers to make CI green. Exact package pins do not stop Dependabot from
proposing new versions. This optional rule keeps automated version PRs within the current React Native series:

```yaml
# Under the relevant npm entry in updates:
ignore:
  - dependency-name: react-native
    update-types:
      - version-update:semver-minor
      - version-update:semver-major
```

This is a version-update policy, not an auto-merge rule. See
[Dependabot's ignore options](https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/reference/supply-chain-security/dependabot-options-reference#ignore).
Review series upgrades separately when the supported host and its React/SDK requirements permit them.

For a candidate update, run ordinary `npm ci`, `npm run typecheck`, the plugin tests, and `ppd check` against
the supported Paseo host. Then install into an isolated daemon and exercise the actual UI. Check a native
client for changes to native components or behavior. `ppd check` includes compilation and load checks; it
does not run TypeScript or prove every render path works. When targeting an older host, set `HERMESC` and
`HERMES` to that host's engine: discovery otherwise prefers binaries shipped with the plugin's local React
Native dependency.

### Observed upgrade experiment

On 2026-10-22, an isolated copy of Paseo Plans (`8bbe518`) was tested against the pinned Paseo 0.12.0
host (`cafe4b0`), which uses React 19.1.0 and React Native 0.81.5:

| Experiment | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Minimal dependency example: RN 0.87.1 with React 19.2.3 | Ordinary lockfile resolution passed |
| The same example with only RN changed to 0.88.0 | Resolution failed: RN requires React `^19.3.0` |
| Plans with RN 0.88.0 and its original React 19.1.0 | Ordinary installation failed with `ERESOLVE` |
| Diagnostic copy with peers bypassed and required test-tooling peers restored | Typecheck failed on changed RN `View` and `ScrollView` ref types, including `focus` and `scrollTo`; 255 tests passed, one approximation test skipped |
| Same diagnostic copy, pinned host compiler and target-host Hermes | 13 checks passed; client and server bundle hashes were unchanged by the RN development dependency bump |

The bypass was an experiment, not an accepted dependency configuration. An initial bypass also removed
automatically installed SDK and Vitest peers; those missing tooling dependencies were restored before
attributing the remaining type errors to React Native. The test shows why passing unit tests and host
compilation cannot replace ordinary dependency resolution and TypeScript. It does not establish RN 0.88
runtime compatibility: the host renderer remained RN 0.81.5, no native host upgrade was attempted, and
no user daemon or production dependency tree was changed. This experiment does not cover other plugins
already adapted to RN 0.87.1 or newer host versions, and is not a universal RN compatibility range.

<h2 id="relies-on">What ppd relies on</h2>

| Relies on | Used by | When it changes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `compiler.js`, `bundle-evaluator.js` and `manifest.js` from `@getpaseo/server` inside the app's `app.asar` (`dist/server/server/plugins`) | `ppd check` (real compile and server evaluation) | `paseo-compile` fails with "could not load Paseo's plugin compiler", the expected path, and the verified and found versions. `ppd check --approx` or `PASEO_SOURCE` works around it. |
| The Lucide exports in the app's web bundle (`app-dist/_expo/static/js/web/index-*.js`) | icon checks | `lucide-icons` warns and falls back to the plugin's own `lucide-react-native`. |
| `@getpaseo/client` and `ws` inside `app.asar` | `agentTimeline` | the call fails naming the modules it looked for. |
| `paseo daemon run --home`, `PASEO_HOME`, `PASEO_LISTEN` and the `config.json` keys | `ppd daemon up` | a start failure prints the daemon output and how it was started. |
| Accessible names in the web UI ("New workspace", "Open menu", "Workspace actions", "Search commands") | `ppd web`, `PaseoWeb` | a timeout names the label it waited for. |
| Rules vendored from Paseo (client registration for 0.12 and 0.13 or later, the release that added each newer client method, manifest schema, build options) | every `ppd check` (`manifest`, `client-eval`), the client check in `ppd install` and `ppd dev`, and the approximation's compile | drift shows up in the weekly CI job against Paseo main. |

`ppd daemon up` prints a note when the installed Paseo is not {meta.verifiedPaseo}, and `ppd doctor` shows the same text
as a warning on its `paseo CLI` line:

```text
note:  ppd was verified on Paseo <verified>; this is <found>
```

<h2 id="upstream">Tracking Paseo main</h2>

The devkit's CI runs its test suite every week against a fresh build of Paseo main (`PASEO_SOURCE`), and
[`examples/ci.yml`](https://github.com/nkwib/paseo-plugin-devkit/blob/main/examples/ci.yml) does the same for your
plugin. A second weekly job fails once Paseo's latest release is no longer {meta.verifiedPaseo}, until ppd is verified on
the new one. When something breaks, an issue with the message helps:
[nkwib/paseo-plugin-devkit/issues](https://github.com/nkwib/paseo-plugin-devkit/issues).
